## The question you're answering

A stock management system needs to store the product code, name, price,
and quantity for every item in a warehouse of 10,000 SKUs. Name one
data structure suitable for this task and explain your choice. (4 marks)

Before we look at anyone's answer, tell me what the question is actually
asking. It's not "name a data structure." It's "name one, and explain
why it's the right one for this specific job." The scenario is doing
work. Ten thousand items. Four different fields per item. The question
is testing whether you can see both halves: many items, each with
structure.

If you miss that the scenario matters, you'll write a textbook
definition of an array and wonder why you got two marks instead of four.

Throughout the three answers below, the prose is highlighted to show
the Answer Arc structure inline. The {% role name="name" %}Name{% /role %}
is tinted green, the {% role name="explain" %}Explain{% /role %} is
tinted gold, and the {% role name="impact" %}Impact{% /role %} is
tinted orange. Where a colour is missing, the corresponding component
is missing or weak in that answer.

## Answer One: the one that leaves marks on the table

> {% role name="name" %}A list could be used to store the data{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}because lists can hold lots of
> items and you can add and remove things from them easily.{% /role %} Lists are
> useful in programming and most languages support them.

Let me tell you what a marker sees when they read this.

**Name.** You said "list." Fine. That's a data structure, mark earned.
Not the best name for what the question wants, but it's a name.

**Explain.** "Lists can hold lots of items and you can add and remove
things from them easily." That's a description of a list in general.
It's not an explanation of why a list fits *this* problem. The fact
that lists are mutable is irrelevant to the scenario. Nobody asked
about adding and removing. The question is about storing 10,000 items
each of which has four fields.

**Impact.** There isn't one. The answer ends on "most languages support
them" which is about the programming ecosystem, not about the stock
management system.

This is the classic pattern I see from students who know the content
but haven't trained the structure. You knew what a list was. You did
not answer the question that was asked. You gave me a textbook entry
on lists. The marker cannot award you marks for the textbook entry;
they can only award marks for your answer to the scenario in front of
you.

Marks this would earn: 1 out of 4.

## Answer Two: the one that gets close and stops

> {% role name="name" %}An array of records would be suitable.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}An array is a structure that
> stores many items of the same type indexed by position, and a record
> stores several fields together under one name. So you would have
> 10,000 records in the array, each record holding the code, name,
> price, and quantity for one item.{% /role %}

Better. Much better. Let's see what it's doing.

**Name.** "An array of records." That's the answer. You've identified
that the scenario needs both multiplicity (many items) and structure
(four fields per item), and you've named the combination. Full marks
for Name.

**Explain.** You've defined both pieces. An array indexes by position;
a record groups fields by name. You've then applied that to the
scenario: 10,000 records in the array, each holding the four fields.
This is a proper explanation. It's not just defining the terms in
isolation; it's showing me how they compose to fit the problem.

**Impact.** This is where it stops. The answer ends with the
composition and doesn't tell me *what the stock management system can
now do* that it couldn't do otherwise.

This is the mark that gets left on the table more than any other, and
it's the reason the Answer Arc framework was built the way it was. You
named it right. You explained it right. You stopped before the
consequence.

Marks this would earn: 3 out of 4.

## Answer Three: the full four marks

> {% role name="name" %}An array of records is suitable.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}An array holds many values indexed
> by position, and a record groups several fields of different types
> under one name, so you would have 10,000 records in the array with
> each record holding the four fields (code, name, price, quantity)
> for one SKU.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}This means the stock management system can iterate over
> all items in a single loop to perform bulk operations like stock
> valuation, low-stock detection, or reorder triggers, which would be
> impossible to manage with 40,000 separate variables and would
> require changing the source code every time a new SKU was added.{% /role %}

Walk through it with me.

**Name.** Same as Answer Two. "Array of records." Clean.

**Explain.** Same structure, slightly more efficient phrasing. "Many
values indexed by position," "fields of different types under one
name," then the composition: 10,000 records, each holding four fields,
one per SKU. The scenario detail is back in the answer, which is where
it should be.

**Impact.** Here's where the mark is won. The answer does three things
that the middling answer didn't.

It says what the system can do. "Iterate over all items in a single
loop." That's a concrete technical capability.

It gives examples of what that capability enables. "Stock valuation,
low-stock detection, reorder triggers." These are business operations.
The answer has crossed from computer science into the domain the
scenario set up.

It says what the alternative would cost. "Impossible to manage with
40,000 separate variables and would require changing the source code
every time a new SKU was added." The negative case sharpens the
positive case. Without the array of records, you don't have a stock
management system, you have a maintenance nightmare.

Marks this would earn: 4 out of 4.

## What to take from all three

The gap between one mark and three marks is whether you paid attention
to the scenario when you wrote the answer. The gap between three marks
and four marks is whether you wrote the Impact. If you only remember
one thing from this exercise, let it be this: the Impact is not
optional padding at the end of your answer. It is the mark most
students don't write, and it is the mark that separates a merit from
a distinction across the whole paper.

When you sit down to write an extended answer in the exam, write NAME,
EXPLAIN, and IMPACT in the margin of your rough paper before you
start the answer. Cross them off as you hit them. If you hit the end
of your answer and you haven't crossed off Impact, you're not
finished.
